sum='Richard H. Ebright collected butterflies from kindergarten; by the second grade he had collected all twenty-five species around his hometown. His mother encouraged him, bought him microscopes and books. A book "The Travels of Monarch X" made him interested in tagging monarch butterflies for Dr Frederick Urquhart. In the seventh grade he did not win at the science fair – he learned that real experiments are needed, not just displays. He then did projects: viceroy butterflies copy monarchs (birds avoid monarchs); gold spots on the monarch pupa produce a hormone needed for the butterfly\'s full development. He won prizes and worked in an army lab. Later he proposed a theory of how cells read their DNA. He was also a debater, public speaker and canoeist. Qualities: a first-rate mind, curiosity and the will to win for the right reasons.',
